 "answer": "# Part A: 86^899 mod 233\n\nSince 233 is prime, by Fermat's Little Theorem, 86^232 \u2261 1 (mod 233).\n\n899 mod 232 = 899 \u2212 3(232) = 203, so we need 86^203 mod 233.\n\nUsing repeated squaring:\n- 86^1 = 86\n- 86^2 = 173\n- 86^4 = 105\n- 86^8 = 74\n- 86^16 = 117\n- 86^32 = 175\n- 86^64 = 102\n- 86^128 = 152\n\nSince 203 = 128+64+8+2+1:\n\n86^203 \u2261 152\u00b7102\u00b774\u00b7173\u00b786 (mod 233)\n\n- 152\u00b7102 = 15504 \u2261 126\n- 126\u00b774 = 9324 \u2261 4\n- 4\u00b7173 = 692 \u2261 226\n- 226\u00b786 = 19436 \u2261 97\n\n**Result: 97**\n\n# Part B: 93137 \u00d7 4819 \u00d7 381 \u00d7 6068\n\nStep 1: 93137 \u00d7 4819 = 448,827,203\n\nStep 2: 448,827,203 \u00d7 381 = 171,003,164,343\n\nStep 3: 171,003,164,343 \u00d7 6068 = 1,037,647,201,233,324\n\n(Checked via mod 9: product \u2261 5\u00b74\u00b73\u00b72 \u2261 3 (mod 9); digit sum of result must also be \u2261 3 mod 9 \u2014 confirmed below.)\n\nDigit sum of 1,037,647,201,233,324:\n1+0+3+7+6+4+7+2+0+1+2+3+3+3+2+4 = 48\n\n(48 \u2192 4+8=12 \u2192 1+2=3, consistent with mod 9 check.)\n\n**Result: 48**\n\n# Part C: Integers 1\u201320000 divisible by 3, 5, or 7 but not by 13\n\n**Step 1: Count divisible by at least one of 3,5,7 (inclusion-exclusion)**\n\n- \u230a20000/3\u230b=6666, \u230a20000/5\u230b=4000, \u230a20000/7\u230b=2857\n- \u230a20000/15\u230b=1333, \u230a20000/21\u230b=952, \u230a20000/35\u230b=571\n- \u230a20000/105\u230b=190\n\nTotal = 6666+4000+2857 \u2212 1333\u2212952\u2212571 + 190 = **10857**\n\n**Step 2: Count those also divisible by 13 (to subtract)**\n\nUsing intersections with 13:\n- \u230a20000/39\u230b=512, \u230a20000/65\u230b=307, \u230a20000/91\u230b=219 \u2192 sum=1038\n- \u230a20000/195\u230b=102, \u230a20000/273\u230b=73, \u230a20000/455\u230b=43 \u2192 sum=218\n- \u230a20000/1365\u230b=14\n\nTotal divisible by (3 or 5 or 7) **and** 13 = 1038 \u2212 218 + 14 = **834**\n\n**Step 3: Final count**\n\n10857 \u2212 834 = **10023**\n\n**Result: 10023**\n\nFINAL: 97;